window.CIVIL_SET_DATA["past-14"] = {"title":"Past Paper Set 14","negativeMarking":0,"chapters":[{"name":"Basic Civil Engineering","questions":[{"id":"PAST-14-005","src":"PAST-14-005","text":"The maximum depth of a timber batten is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"30 mm"},{"key":"b","text":"50 mm"},{"key":"c","text":"60 mm"},{"key":"d","text":"100 mm"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"Battens are sawn timber pieces of small section. Their depth (thickness) does not exceed about 50 mm, while their width is under about 200 mm.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":5,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q5 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0101"},{"id":"PAST-14-020","src":"PAST-14-020","text":"The objective of seasoning of timber is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"to reduce moisture content"},{"key":"b","text":"to increase the structural stability of timber"},{"key":"c","text":"increasing the resistance to shrinkage, cracking, warping and even decay"},{"key":"d","text":"all"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"Seasoning brings the moisture content near equilibrium. This gives dimensional stability, strength and resistance to shrinkage, cracking, warping and decay.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":20,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q20 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0101"},{"id":"PAST-14-025","src":"PAST-14-025","text":"The centre of gravity of a solid hemisphere lies on the central radius","options":[{"key":"a","text":"at distance 3r/2 from the plane base"},{"key":"b","text":"at distance 3r/4 from the plane base"},{"key":"c","text":"at distance 3r/5 from the plane base"},{"key":"d","text":"at distance 3r/8 from the plane base"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"The centroid of a solid hemisphere is 3r/8 from its plane face along the axis of symmetry.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":25,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q25 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0104"},{"id":"PAST-14-026","src":"PAST-14-026","text":"The removal of impurities from ore is known as","options":[{"key":"a","text":"crushing and grinding"},{"key":"b","text":"concentration of ore"},{"key":"c","text":"minerals"},{"key":"d","text":"gangue"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"Removing the gangue (earthy impurities) from an ore is called concentration or dressing of the ore, for example by washing, magnetic separation, froth flotation or leaching.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":26,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q26 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0101"},{"id":"PAST-14-028","src":"PAST-14-028","text":"With which compound of cement does gypsum react to reduce the flash setting of cement?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"C<sub>3</sub>A"},{"key":"b","text":"C<sub>3</sub>S"},{"key":"c","text":"C<sub>2</sub>S"},{"key":"d","text":"C<sub>4</sub>AF"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Gypsum reacts with tricalcium aluminate to form ettringite, which coats the C<sub>3</sub>A grains and retards their instant (flash) set.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":28,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q28 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0101"},{"id":"PAST-14-033","src":"PAST-14-033","text":"The following data are obtained from levelling: 4.25, 6.81, 2.35, 6.35, 6.25, 4.35, 2.6. The instrument was shifted after the 3rd and 5th readings. What are the 2.35 and 6.25 readings?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"BS &amp; FS"},{"key":"b","text":"BS &amp; IS"},{"key":"c","text":"IS &amp; FS"},{"key":"d","text":"Both b and c"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"The 3rd (2.35) and 5th (6.25) readings are taken just before the instrument is shifted, so they are foresights on change points. The next readings (6.35 and 4.35) are backsights on the same points. A change point carries both a BS and an FS, which is the key's (a).","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":33,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q33 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0105"},{"id":"PAST-14-043","src":"PAST-14-043","text":"Cast iron and wrought iron contain carbon up to","options":[{"key":"a","text":"2–4%, &lt;0.08%"},{"key":"b","text":"2–4%, 0.08–2%"},{"key":"c","text":"4%, &gt;0.08%"},{"key":"d","text":"None"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Cast iron contains about 2–4.5% carbon. Wrought iron is almost pure iron, with very little carbon (below about 0.1%).","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":43,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q43 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0101"},{"id":"PAST-14-046","src":"PAST-14-046","text":"The advantage of a clamp compared to a kiln for burning bricks is that","options":[{"key":"a","text":"it takes less time for burning"},{"key":"b","text":"it gives more output of first class bricks"},{"key":"c","text":"it has less initial cost"},{"key":"d","text":"it is suitable when bricks are required in large numbers"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"A clamp is a temporary pile of bricks and fuel with almost no initial cost. It burns slowly and unevenly, however, giving fewer first-class bricks than a kiln.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":46,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q46 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0101"},{"id":"PAST-14-047","src":"PAST-14-047","text":"Which of the following cements contains the maximum percentage of di-calcium silicate?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Ordinary Portland cement"},{"key":"b","text":"Low heat cement"},{"key":"c","text":"Rapid hardening cement"},{"key":"d","text":"Sulphate resisting cement"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"Low-heat cement has more C<sub>2</sub>S (and less C<sub>3</sub>S and C<sub>3</sub>A), so it hydrates slowly with little heat. This suits mass concrete.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":47,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q47 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0101"},{"id":"PAST-14-048","src":"PAST-14-048","text":"The type of bond provided in brick masonry for carrying heavy loads is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"single Flemish bond"},{"key":"b","text":"double Flemish bond"},{"key":"c","text":"English bond"},{"key":"d","text":"zigzag bond"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"English bond (alternate courses of headers and stretchers) has no continuous vertical joints. It is the strongest bond and is used for load-bearing walls.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":48,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q48 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0103"},{"id":"PAST-14-049","src":"PAST-14-049","text":"The art and science of determining the relative positions of various points or stations located on the surface of the earth is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"surveying"},{"key":"b","text":"levelling"},{"key":"c","text":"both A and B"},{"key":"d","text":"none of the above"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Surveying fixes the relative positions of points, horizontally and vertically. Levelling is the branch that deals only with relative heights.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":49,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q49 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0105"},{"id":"PAST-14-064","src":"PAST-14-064","text":"A channel (C) section has flanges 5 cm wide, an overall depth of 10 cm and a uniform thickness of 1.5 cm. Its centroid (x̄ from the back of the web, ȳ from the bottom) is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"1.70 cm, 5 cm"},{"key":"b","text":"1.78 cm, 5 cm"},{"key":"c","text":"178 mm, 5 mm"},{"key":"d","text":"2 cm, 5 cm"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"The flanges (2 × 5 × 1.5 = 15 cm<sup>2</sup> at x = 2.5) and web (7 × 1.5 = 10.5 cm<sup>2</sup> at x = 0.75) give x̄ = (37.5 + 7.9)/25.5 ≈ 1.78 cm. By symmetry ȳ = 5 cm. (The paper shows these dimensions on a sketch.)","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":64,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q64 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0104"}]},{"name":"Soil Mechanics and Foundation Engineering","questions":[{"id":"PAST-14-002","src":"PAST-14-002","text":"The size of footings depends upon","options":[{"key":"a","text":"type of load"},{"key":"b","text":"type of superstructure"},{"key":"c","text":"bearing capacity of soil"},{"key":"d","text":"both (a) and (c)"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"Footing area = load / allowable bearing pressure, so it depends on the magnitude and type of load and on the bearing capacity of the soil.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":2,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q2 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0205"},{"id":"PAST-14-032","src":"PAST-14-032","text":"The ultimate bearing capacity of soil is the","options":[{"key":"a","text":"load at which it fails"},{"key":"b","text":"load at which it consolidates"},{"key":"c","text":"safe allowable load"},{"key":"d","text":"load below the elastic limit"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Ultimate bearing capacity is the least gross pressure at which the soil fails in shear. The safe bearing capacity is this divided by a factor of safety.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":32,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q32 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0206"},{"id":"PAST-14-034","src":"PAST-14-034","text":"What is the effect of water table fluctuation above ground level on effective stress?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"No effect"},{"key":"b","text":"Increases"},{"key":"c","text":"Decreases"},{"key":"d","text":"None of the above"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"When the water stands above the ground, a change in its level alters the total stress and the pore pressure equally, so the effective stress in the soil does not change.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":34,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q34 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0202"},{"id":"PAST-14-038","src":"PAST-14-038","text":"The falling head permeability test is done in the case of","options":[{"key":"a","text":"cohesive or less permeable soils"},{"key":"b","text":"cohesionless and more permeable soils"},{"key":"c","text":"both (a) and (b)"},{"key":"d","text":"none of the above"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"In fine or less permeable soils the flow is too small to measure at constant head, so the falling (variable) head test is used. Constant head tests suit sands and gravels.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":38,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q38 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0201"},{"id":"PAST-14-050","src":"PAST-14-050","text":"According to USCS, fine grained soils are classified on the basis of","options":[{"key":"a","text":"plasticity"},{"key":"b","text":"grain size distribution"},{"key":"c","text":"group index"},{"key":"d","text":"particle size composition"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Fine-grained soils (over 50% passing 75 µm) are classified by their liquid limit and plasticity index on Casagrande's plasticity chart (ML, CL, MH, CH…).","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":50,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q50 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0201"},{"id":"PAST-14-051","src":"PAST-14-051","text":"Every process involving a decrease in the water content of a saturated soil without replacement of the water by air is called","options":[{"key":"a","text":"compression"},{"key":"b","text":"compressibility"},{"key":"c","text":"tension"},{"key":"d","text":"consolidation"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"Terzaghi's definition: consolidation is any process that reduces the water content of a saturated soil without air replacing the water, i.e. the soil stays saturated as it compresses.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":51,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q51 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0206"}]},{"name":"Basic Water Resources Engineering","questions":[{"id":"PAST-14-006","src":"PAST-14-006","text":"For supercritical flow, the Froude number should be","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Fr &lt; 1"},{"key":"b","text":"Fr = 1"},{"key":"c","text":"Fr &gt; 1"},{"key":"d","text":"None"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"Supercritical flow has Fr = V/√(gD) &gt; 1. Subcritical flow has Fr &lt; 1 and critical flow Fr = 1.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":6,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q6 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0305"},{"id":"PAST-14-013","src":"PAST-14-013","text":"The depth of flow at which the specific energy is minimum and the discharge is maximum is called","options":[{"key":"a","text":"critical depth"},{"key":"b","text":"sequent depth"},{"key":"c","text":"conjugate depth"},{"key":"d","text":"normal depth"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"At the critical depth the specific energy is minimum for a given discharge, and the discharge is maximum for a given specific energy (Fr = 1).","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":13,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q13 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0305"},{"id":"PAST-14-018","src":"PAST-14-018","text":"For steady, incompressible 2-D flow, the continuity equation is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"∂u/∂x + ∂v/∂y + ∂w/∂z = 0"},{"key":"b","text":"∂(ρu)/∂x + ∂(ρv)/∂y = 0"},{"key":"c","text":"v∂u/∂x + u∂v/∂y = 0"},{"key":"d","text":"∂u/∂x + ∂v/∂y = 0"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"For incompressible two-dimensional flow the divergence of velocity is zero: ∂u/∂x + ∂v/∂y = 0. Option (a) is the 3-D form and (b) the compressible form.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":18,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q18 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0303"},{"id":"PAST-14-035","src":"PAST-14-035","text":"Non-ideal flow","options":[{"key":"a","text":"follows Newton's law of viscosity"},{"key":"b","text":"does not follow Newton's law of viscosity"},{"key":"c","text":"both (a) and (b)"},{"key":"d","text":"none of the above"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"Non-ideal (real) fluids have viscosity. Some obey Newton's law of viscosity (Newtonian fluids such as water and air) and some do not (non-Newtonian fluids), so both can occur.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":35,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q35 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0301"},{"id":"PAST-14-036","src":"PAST-14-036","text":"What is the Laplace equation (for 2-D flow in the x–z plane)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"None of these"},{"key":"b","text":"∂<sup>2</sup>φ/∂x<sup>2</sup> + ∂<sup>2</sup>φ/∂z<sup>2</sup> = 0"},{"key":"c","text":"∂φ/∂x + ∂<sup>2</sup>φ/∂y<sup>2</sup> = 0"},{"key":"d","text":"∂<sup>2</sup>φ/∂x<sup>2</sup> + ∂<sup>2</sup>φ/∂z = 0"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"For irrotational incompressible 2-D flow, the potential satisfies ∇<sup>2</sup>φ = ∂<sup>2</sup>φ/∂x<sup>2</sup> + ∂<sup>2</sup>φ/∂z<sup>2</sup> = 0. (The paper prints options (a) and (b) identically; (a) is shown here as 'None of these'.)","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":36,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q36 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0303"},{"id":"PAST-14-053","src":"PAST-14-053","text":"Which among the following is an assumption of the Hagen-Poiseuille equation?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Fluid is uniform"},{"key":"b","text":"Flow is laminar"},{"key":"c","text":"Flow is turbulent"},{"key":"d","text":"Fluid is compressible"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"The Hagen–Poiseuille equation h<sub>f</sub> = 32μVL/(ρgD<sup>2</sup>) assumes steady, fully developed laminar flow of an incompressible Newtonian fluid in a circular pipe.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":53,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q53 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0304"},{"id":"PAST-14-054","src":"PAST-14-054","text":"The drag coefficient of a complete structure such as an aircraft includes","options":[{"key":"a","text":"form drag"},{"key":"b","text":"pressure drag"},{"key":"c","text":"interference drag"},{"key":"d","text":"induced drag"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"Summing the component drags does not account for their mutual interaction. The drag coefficient of the complete body therefore also includes interference drag at the junctions of parts.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":54,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q54 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0303"},{"id":"PAST-14-055","src":"PAST-14-055","text":"Jet propulsion of a ship in very shallow water is needed to","options":[{"key":"a","text":"avoid sinking of the ship"},{"key":"b","text":"avoid damage of the propeller"},{"key":"c","text":"avoid current directions"},{"key":"d","text":"avoid surface damage"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"In very shallow water an exposed screw propeller would strike the bed and be damaged. A water-jet propulsion system avoids this.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":55,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q55 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0303"},{"id":"PAST-14-056","src":"PAST-14-056","text":"The fundamental dimensional quantities are related by","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Avogadro's law"},{"key":"b","text":"Newton's second law"},{"key":"c","text":"Newton's first law"},{"key":"d","text":"Newton's third law"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"Newton's second law F = ma links force to mass, length and time (F = MLT<sup>−2</sup>). This allows either the MLT or the FLT system of fundamental dimensions.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":56,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q56 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0301"},{"id":"PAST-14-066","src":"PAST-14-066","text":"A catchment of 225 ha area has a runoff coefficient of 0.33. A storm of duration more than the time of concentration and intensity 7.78 cm/hr occurs over the area. Find the peak discharge by the rational method.","options":[{"key":"a","text":"16 m<sup>3</sup>/s"},{"key":"b","text":"1600 L/sec"},{"key":"c","text":"160 cm<sup>3</sup>/s"},{"key":"d","text":"16000 L/min"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Q = CIA/360 with I in mm/h and A in ha: 0.33 × 77.8 × 225/360 ≈ 16 m<sup>3</sup>/s.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":66,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q66 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0306"},{"id":"PAST-14-067","src":"PAST-14-067","text":"Find the discharge through a rectangular orifice 3.2 m wide and 1.7 m deep fitted to a water tank. The water level in the tank is 3.3 m above the top edge of the orifice. Take C<sub>d</sub> = 0.6.","options":[{"key":"a","text":"29.4 m<sup>3</sup>/s"},{"key":"b","text":"58.5 m<sup>3</sup>/s"},{"key":"c","text":"67.9 m<sup>3</sup>/s"},{"key":"d","text":"78.7 m<sup>3</sup>/s"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"For a large orifice, Q = (2/3)C<sub>d</sub>b√(2g)(H<sub>2</sub><sup>3/2</sup> − H<sub>1</sub><sup>3/2</sup>) with H<sub>1</sub> = 3.3 m and H<sub>2</sub> = 5.0 m. This gives 0.4 × 3.2 × 4.43 × (11.18 − 5.99) ≈ 29.4 m<sup>3</sup>/s.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":67,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q67 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0303"},{"id":"PAST-14-070","src":"PAST-14-070","text":"What is the pressure at 1 m below the ground water (table)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"9.81 N/m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"b","text":"9.81 kN/m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"c","text":"10 N/m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"d","text":"25 N/m<sup>2</sup>"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"The pore water pressure is u = γ<sub>w</sub>h = 9.81 × 1 = 9.81 kN/m<sup>2</sup>.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":70,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q70 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0302"},{"id":"PAST-14-078","src":"PAST-14-078","text":"The dimensions of a rectangular channel are 3 m in depth and 4 m in width. Calculate the bed slope of the channel if the specific energy is 3.13 m (C = 50).","options":[{"key":"a","text":"1 in 1000"},{"key":"b","text":"1 in 1100"},{"key":"c","text":"1 in 1200"},{"key":"d","text":"1 in 1300"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"V<sup>2</sup>/2g = 3.13 − 3 = 0.13, so V ≈ 1.60 m/s. R = 12/10 = 1.2 m, and S = V<sup>2</sup>/(C<sup>2</sup>R) = 2.55/(2500 × 1.2) ≈ 1/1176 ≈ 1 in 1200.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":78,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q78 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0305"}]},{"name":"Structural Mechanics","questions":[{"id":"PAST-14-007","src":"PAST-14-007","text":"The shear stress in a beam is …… on the NA.","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Maximum"},{"key":"b","text":"Minimum"},{"key":"c","text":"Zero"},{"key":"d","text":"None of the above"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"τ = VQ/Ib, and the first moment Q is largest at the neutral axis, so the shear stress is maximum there and zero at the extreme fibres.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":7,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q7 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0403"},{"id":"PAST-14-011","src":"PAST-14-011","text":"In a simply supported beam, a point load acts at the centre of the span. What will be the strain energy?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"W<sup>2</sup>l<sup>3</sup>/48EI"},{"key":"b","text":"W<sup>2</sup>l<sup>2</sup>/48EI"},{"key":"c","text":"W<sup>2</sup>l<sup>3</sup>/96EI"},{"key":"d","text":"W<sup>2</sup>l<sup>2</sup>/96EI"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"U = (1/2)Wδ = (1/2)W × Wl<sup>3</sup>/48EI = W<sup>2</sup>l<sup>3</sup>/96EI.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":11,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q11 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0402"},{"id":"PAST-14-015","src":"PAST-14-015","text":"The value of Poisson's ratio would be","options":[{"key":"a","text":"0"},{"key":"b","text":"&gt;1"},{"key":"c","text":"&lt;1"},{"key":"d","text":"Constant"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"For engineering materials, Poisson's ratio lies between 0 and 0.5 (about 0.25–0.3 for steel, 0.15–0.2 for concrete), so it is always less than 1.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":15,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q15 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0402"},{"id":"PAST-14-017","src":"PAST-14-017","text":"The distribution factor is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"the ratio of stiffness of member and member"},{"key":"b","text":"the ratio of stiffness of near joint and far joint"},{"key":"c","text":"the ratio of stiffness of member and joint (sum of member stiffness)"},{"key":"d","text":"the ratio of stiffness of joint and member"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"DF = stiffness of the member ÷ sum of the stiffnesses of all members meeting at the joint. The DFs at a joint add up to 1.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":17,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q17 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0406"},{"id":"PAST-14-022","src":"PAST-14-022","text":"If the shear force is zero, then the bending moment will be","options":[{"key":"a","text":"maximum"},{"key":"b","text":"minimum"},{"key":"c","text":"either maximum or minimum"},{"key":"d","text":"zero"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"Because dM/dx = V, the moment has a stationary value (local maximum or minimum) where the shear force is zero or changes sign.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":22,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q22 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0401"},{"id":"PAST-14-057","src":"PAST-14-057","text":"A column has a slenderness (stiffness) of 25. It fails under load by","options":[{"key":"a","text":"crushing"},{"key":"b","text":"bending"},{"key":"c","text":"shear"},{"key":"d","text":"buckling"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"The key treats a column with a slenderness ratio of 25 as short, so it fails by direct crushing. Long columns with high slenderness fail by buckling.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":57,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q57 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0403"},{"id":"PAST-14-058","src":"PAST-14-058","text":"A plane truss has 11 members and 8 joints. The number of additional members needed to make the truss stable is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"0"},{"key":"b","text":"1"},{"key":"c","text":"2"},{"key":"d","text":"3"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"A simple truss needs m = 2j − 3 = 13 members, so 2 more are required.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":58,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q58 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0405"},{"id":"PAST-14-059","src":"PAST-14-059","text":"An increase in temperature …… the stress in determinate structures.","options":[{"key":"a","text":"decreases"},{"key":"b","text":"increases"},{"key":"c","text":"does not change"},{"key":"d","text":"doubles"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"Determinate structures can expand freely, so a uniform temperature change causes deformation but no stresses. Indeterminate structures develop temperature stresses.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":59,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q59 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0405"},{"id":"PAST-14-060","src":"PAST-14-060","text":"ILDs of statically determinate structures can be","options":[{"key":"a","text":"straight lines only"},{"key":"b","text":"curved lines only"},{"key":"c","text":"straight lines and curves"},{"key":"d","text":"none"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"By the Müller-Breslau principle, the ILDs of determinate structures are made up of straight lines, since they are rigid-body displacement shapes. ILDs of indeterminate structures are curved.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":60,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q60 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0404"},{"id":"PAST-14-065","src":"PAST-14-065","text":"A cantilever carries point loads of 300 kN, 500 kN and 800 kN at 0.5 m, 2 m and 4 m from the fixed support. Find the shear force and bending moment at the support.","options":[{"key":"a","text":"1600 kN, 4350 kNm"},{"key":"b","text":"1600 N, 4350 Nm"},{"key":"c","text":"1680 kN, 4200 kNm"},{"key":"d","text":"1450 kN, 4350 kNm"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"V = 300 + 500 + 800 = 1600 kN and M = 300×0.5 + 500×2 + 800×4 = 4350 kNm. (The paper gives the beam only as a figure; the loads are those used in the key's working.)","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":65,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q65 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0401"},{"id":"PAST-14-073","src":"PAST-14-073","text":"At a certain point in a strained material, two direct stresses of 80 N/mm<sup>2</sup> (tensile) and 60 N/mm<sup>2</sup> (tensile) are acting along with a shear stress of 20 N/mm<sup>2</sup>. Find the major principal stress.","options":[{"key":"a","text":"90 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"b","text":"92.2 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"c","text":"92.36 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"d","text":"96 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"σ<sub>1</sub> = (80+60)/2 + √[((80−60)/2)<sup>2</sup> + 20<sup>2</sup>] = 70 + √500 ≈ 92.36 N/mm<sup>2</sup>.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":73,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q73 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0402"},{"id":"PAST-14-076","src":"PAST-14-076","text":"The maximum shear stress in a beam of width 300 mm and depth 450 mm with 50 kN shear force at that section is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"0.370 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"b","text":"0.556 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"c","text":"0.740 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"d","text":"0.037 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"For a rectangle, τ<sub>max</sub> = 1.5V/(bd) = 1.5 × 50,000/(300 × 450) ≈ 0.556 N/mm<sup>2</sup>.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":76,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q76 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0403"},{"id":"PAST-14-080","src":"PAST-14-080","text":"The stiffness at the near (free) end of a member if the far end is fixed is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"4EI/L"},{"key":"b","text":"3EI/L"},{"key":"c","text":"2EI/L"},{"key":"d","text":"EI/L"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Rotating the near end through a unit angle with the far end fixed needs 4EI/L. It is 3EI/L if the far end is hinged.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":80,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q80 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0406"}]},{"name":"Design of Structures","questions":[{"id":"PAST-14-023","src":"PAST-14-023","text":"What is the formula to calculate wind pressure?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"0.6 V<sub>z</sub><sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"b","text":"0.5 V<sub>z</sub><sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"c","text":"0.6 V<sub>z</sub><sup>3</sup>"},{"key":"d","text":"0.5 V<sub>z</sub><sup>3</sup>"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"IS 875 (Part 3): p<sub>z</sub> = 0.6V<sub>z</sub><sup>2</sup> N/m<sup>2</sup>, with V<sub>z</sub> the design wind speed in m/s.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":23,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q23 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0501"},{"id":"PAST-14-031","src":"PAST-14-031","text":"Formwork of a column is removed after ……","options":[{"key":"a","text":"12 hrs"},{"key":"b","text":"24 hrs"},{"key":"c","text":"3 days"},{"key":"d","text":"7 days"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"IS 456 allows the vertical formwork of columns, walls and beam sides to be struck after 16–24 hours.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":31,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q31 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0502"},{"id":"PAST-14-061","src":"PAST-14-061","text":"Details about wind load are given in code","options":[{"key":"a","text":"NBC 103"},{"key":"b","text":"NBC 104"},{"key":"c","text":"NBC 105"},{"key":"d","text":"NBC 106"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"NBC 104 covers wind loads. NBC 103 covers imposed loads, NBC 105 seismic design and NBC 106 snow loads.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":61,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q61 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0501"},{"id":"PAST-14-071","src":"PAST-14-071","text":"If the web area of a deep beam is 180000 mm<sup>2</sup>, the minimum side face reinforcement is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"180 mm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"b","text":"1800 mm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"c","text":"240 mm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"d","text":"2400 mm<sup>2</sup>"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"IS 456 (cl. 26.5.1.3) requires side-face steel of 0.1% of the web area, split equally between the two faces: 0.001 × 180,000 = 180 mm<sup>2</sup>.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":71,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q71 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0503"},{"id":"PAST-14-077","src":"PAST-14-077","text":"For a square footing of effective depth 500 mm and sides of 3 m, supporting a column of width 500 mm, what is the critical area to be considered for two way shear?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"4.00 m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"b","text":"2.00 m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"c","text":"6.75 m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"d","text":"1.50 m<sup>2</sup>"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"The punching-shear perimeter lies d/2 from the column faces: side = 0.5 + 0.5 = 1.0 m, so b<sub>0</sub> = 4 m. The resisting area is b<sub>0</sub>d = 4 × 0.5 = 2.0 m<sup>2</sup>.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":77,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q77 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0503"}]},{"name":"Water Supply, Sanitation and Environment","questions":[{"id":"PAST-14-008","src":"PAST-14-008","text":"Which of the following is not used as a media for trickling filters?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Sand"},{"key":"b","text":"Geotextiles"},{"key":"c","text":"Gravel"},{"key":"d","text":"Paper"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"Trickling filter media are coarse, durable materials such as broken stone, gravel, slag, plastic modules or even geotextiles, on which the biofilm grows. Paper disintegrates and cannot be used.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":8,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q8 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0605"},{"id":"PAST-14-021","src":"PAST-14-021","text":"The maximum alkalinity value is at","options":[{"key":"a","text":"pH = 14"},{"key":"b","text":"pH = 7"},{"key":"c","text":"pH &lt; 7"},{"key":"d","text":"pH &gt; 7"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"On the 0–14 scale, pH 7 is neutral and higher values are more alkaline, so the extreme alkaline end is pH 14.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":21,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q21 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0601"},{"id":"PAST-14-062","src":"PAST-14-062","text":"A sample of ground water at a pH of 7.0 contains 122 mg/L of bicarbonates. What is the alkalinity of this water (in terms of CaCO<sub>3</sub>)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"120 mg/L"},{"key":"b","text":"60 mg/L"},{"key":"c","text":"100 mg/L"},{"key":"d","text":"200 mg/L"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"At pH 7 the alkalinity is all bicarbonate: 122 × (50/61) = 100 mg/L as CaCO<sub>3</sub> (equivalent weights 50 and 61).","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":62,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q62 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0601"},{"id":"PAST-14-063","src":"PAST-14-063","text":"A sewage sample of 3 ml is diluted with distilled water in a 300 ml capacity BOD bottle and incubated at 20 degree Celsius for 5 days, and the depletion of dissolved oxygen (DO) was 10 mg/L. Then, the initial (ultimate) BOD of the sample is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"146.30 mg/l"},{"key":"b","text":"1.463 mg/l"},{"key":"c","text":"1463 mg/l"},{"key":"d","text":"14.63 mg/l"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"BOD<sub>5</sub> = 10 × 300/3 = 1000 mg/L. With k = 0.23/day (base e), L<sub>0</sub> = 1000/(1 − e<sup>−1.15</sup>) ≈ 1463 mg/L.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":63,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q63 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0605"},{"id":"PAST-14-079","src":"PAST-14-079","text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Water can be withdrawn from any selected level of the reservoir using dry intake towers"},{"key":"b","text":"Intake towers have to be designed for the worst combination of hydrostatic, wind, earthquake and wave forces"},{"key":"c","text":"Dry intake towers are lighter in construction as compared to wet intake towers"},{"key":"d","text":"In a dry intake tower, the water is directly drawn into the withdrawal conduit through the gated entry ports"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"A dry intake tower is empty inside, so it must resist buoyancy and full external water pressure. It is therefore heavier, not lighter, than a wet tower.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":79,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q79 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0602"}]},{"name":"Irrigation and Drainage","questions":[{"id":"PAST-14-010","src":"PAST-14-010","text":"If D is the scour depth measured below the bed, then the width of the launching apron will generally be equal to","options":[{"key":"a","text":"1.5D"},{"key":"b","text":"1.25D"},{"key":"c","text":"2D"},{"key":"d","text":"D"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"The launching apron is usually made about 1.5D wide. When the bed scours, it launches down the slope at about 2:1 to cover the scour face.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":10,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q10 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0704"},{"id":"PAST-14-012","src":"PAST-14-012","text":"Head loss will be small in the case of","options":[{"key":"a","text":"ogee shaped"},{"key":"b","text":"broad crested"},{"key":"c","text":"sharp crested"},{"key":"d","text":"narrow crested"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"The key answers the broad-crested weir. Over its long crest the flow becomes nearly parallel at critical depth, with little disturbance and loss.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":12,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q12 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0703"}]},{"name":"Hydropower","questions":[{"id":"PAST-14-001","src":"PAST-14-001","text":"What is the value of eccentricity for the no tension condition in a dam?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"e &lt; B/6"},{"key":"b","text":"e &gt; B/6"},{"key":"c","text":"e &gt; B/3"},{"key":"d","text":"e &lt; B/3"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"The resultant must lie within the middle third of the base (e ≤ B/6). The stresses at toe and heel then stay compressive; at e = B/6 the heel stress is zero.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":1,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q1 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0803"},{"id":"PAST-14-003","src":"PAST-14-003","text":"A turbine develops 10000 hp power under a head of 81 metres at 500 rpm. Recognize the turbine.","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Pelton wheel"},{"key":"b","text":"Francis"},{"key":"c","text":"Kaplan"},{"key":"d","text":"None"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"N<sub>s</sub> = N√P/H<sup>5/4</sup> = 500 × 100/81<sup>1.25</sup> = 50,000/243 ≈ 206. This lies in the Francis range (about 60–300).","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":3,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q3 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0806"},{"id":"PAST-14-004","src":"PAST-14-004","text":"For the empty reservoir condition, the maximum compressive force acts on the","options":[{"key":"a","text":"toe"},{"key":"b","text":"heel"},{"key":"c","text":"both (a) and (b)"},{"key":"d","text":"all of the above"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"With the reservoir empty, only the dam's weight acts. Its resultant falls towards the upstream side, so the maximum compressive stress is at the heel.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":4,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q4 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0803"},{"id":"PAST-14-009","src":"PAST-14-009","text":"An example of an impulse turbine is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Pelton turbine"},{"key":"b","text":"Crossflow turbine"},{"key":"c","text":"Turgo turbine"},{"key":"d","text":"All of the above"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"Pelton, Turgo and cross-flow (Banki) turbines all work with a free jet at atmospheric pressure, so they are impulse turbines.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":9,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q9 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0806"},{"id":"PAST-14-075","src":"PAST-14-075","text":"In an inward flow reaction turbine, the velocity of flow at the inlet is 2 m/s. If the guide vanes make an angle of 30° with the tangential velocity direction of the runner, the absolute velocity of the water leaving the guide vanes is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"1 m/s"},{"key":"b","text":"2 m/s"},{"key":"c","text":"3 m/s"},{"key":"d","text":"4 m/s"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"The flow velocity is the radial component of the absolute velocity: V<sub>f</sub> = V sin30°, so V = 2/0.5 = 4 m/s.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":75,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q75 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0806"}]},{"name":"Transportation","questions":[{"id":"PAST-14-016","src":"PAST-14-016","text":"If the time mean speed is 50 kmph, then the space mean speed is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"more than 50 kmph"},{"key":"b","text":"less than 50 kmph"},{"key":"c","text":"Time mean speed = 2 × space mean speed"},{"key":"d","text":"All of the above"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"Time mean speed over-weights fast vehicles, so space mean speed is always less than or equal to it: v<sub>t</sub> = v<sub>s</sub> + σ<sup>2</sup>/v<sub>s</sub>.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":16,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q16 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0904"},{"id":"PAST-14-019","src":"PAST-14-019","text":"The wheel load is transferred by the","options":[{"key":"a","text":"asphalt"},{"key":"b","text":"subbase"},{"key":"c","text":"subgrade"},{"key":"d","text":"all"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"In a flexible pavement, the load spreads down through the surface (asphalt), base and subbase layers to the subgrade, each layer carrying and dispersing it.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":19,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q19 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0905"},{"id":"PAST-14-024","src":"PAST-14-024","text":"In the CBR test, the penetration values at 2.5 mm and 5 mm are such that","options":[{"key":"a","text":"2.5 mm has higher penetration rate"},{"key":"b","text":"5 mm has higher penetration rate"},{"key":"c","text":"both have equal penetration rate"},{"key":"d","text":"none of the above"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"The key's intent: the CBR value at 2.5 mm is normally higher than at 5 mm and is reported. If the 5 mm value is higher, the test is repeated, and if it is confirmed the 5 mm value is used. (The plunger itself always moves at 1.25 mm/min.)","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":24,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q24 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0905"},{"id":"PAST-14-030","src":"PAST-14-030","text":"Arterial roads are called","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Sadak"},{"key":"b","text":"Marga"},{"key":"c","text":"Upamarga"},{"key":"d","text":"Path"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"In the Nepali road naming hierarchy used in these papers, arterial roads are called Path, with the lower classes named Sadak, Marga and Upamarga.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":30,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q30 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0901"},{"id":"PAST-14-037","src":"PAST-14-037","text":"An exceptional gradient is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"a gradient steeper than the ruling gradient which is used for a limited road length"},{"key":"b","text":"a gradient steeper than the ruling gradient which may be used for a very short length in an exceptional case"},{"key":"c","text":"a maximum gradient within which the designer attempts to design the vertical profile of the road"},{"key":"d","text":"the minimum longitudinal gradient for longitudinal drainage purpose"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"Exceptional gradients are steeper than the limiting gradient and are allowed only over very short stretches in unavoidable situations. The ruling gradient is the normal design maximum.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":37,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q37 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0902"},{"id":"PAST-14-045","src":"PAST-14-045","text":"A traffic sign indicating a speed limit is categorized as a","options":[{"key":"a","text":"regulatory sign"},{"key":"b","text":"warning sign"},{"key":"c","text":"informatory sign"},{"key":"d","text":"prohibitory sign"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Speed limits, stop and give-way signs are regulatory (mandatory) signs that impose legal restrictions. Warning signs alert drivers to hazards.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":45,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q45 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0904"},{"id":"PAST-14-074","src":"PAST-14-074","text":"If the superelevation provided is zero, then the design speed of a highway having a curve of 200 m and coefficient of friction 0.10 is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"40 kmph"},{"key":"b","text":"50 kmph"},{"key":"c","text":"55 kmph"},{"key":"d","text":"60 kmph"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"v = √(127Rf) = √(127 × 200 × 0.10) = √2540 ≈ 50 km/h.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":74,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q74 · 2 marks"},"topic":"ACiE0902"}]},{"name":"Project Planning, Design and Implementation","questions":[{"id":"PAST-14-014","src":"PAST-14-014","text":"The members selected by the Nepal Engineering Council itself are","options":[{"key":"a","text":"2"},{"key":"b","text":"3"},{"key":"c","text":"4"},{"key":"d","text":"5"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Among its members, the NEC Act provides for two engineers nominated by the Council itself.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":14,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q14 · 1 mark"},"topic":"AALL1006"},{"id":"PAST-14-027","src":"PAST-14-027","text":"What is the function of the Nepal Engineering Council?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"To make the profession effective by mobilizing it in a more systematic and scientific way and also to register the engineers as per their qualifications"},{"key":"b","text":"To make the engineering profession effective by mobilizing it in a more systematic and scientific way and also to register the engineers as per their qualifications"},{"key":"c","text":"To punish the engineering profession effective by mobilizing it in a more systematic and scientific way and also to register the engineers as per their qualifications"},{"key":"d","text":"All of the above"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"The NEC Act's preamble states the Council's purpose: to make the engineering profession effective by mobilising it in a more systematic and scientific way, and to register engineers according to their qualifications.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":27,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q27 · 1 mark"},"topic":"AALL1006"},{"id":"PAST-14-029","src":"PAST-14-029","text":"The area of A0 paper is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"1 m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"b","text":"15 m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"c","text":"2 m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"d","text":"1 m<sup>3</sup>"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"A0 (841 × 1189 mm) has an area of 1 m<sup>2</sup>. Each smaller A size is half the area of the one before.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":29,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q29 · 1 mark"},"topic":"AALL1001"},{"id":"PAST-14-039","src":"PAST-14-039","text":"A point 'P' is above the Horizontal Plane (HP) and in front of the Vertical Plane (VP). The point is in the","options":[{"key":"a","text":"first quadrant"},{"key":"b","text":"second quadrant"},{"key":"c","text":"third quadrant"},{"key":"d","text":"fourth quadrant"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Above the HP and in front of the VP is the first quadrant (first-angle projection). Above the HP and behind the VP is the second.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":39,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q39 · 1 mark"},"topic":"AALL1001"},{"id":"PAST-14-040","src":"PAST-14-040","text":"If SPI &lt; 1 and CPI &lt; 1, then which of the following is true?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Project is ahead of schedule and in less budget"},{"key":"b","text":"Project is behind the schedule and over budget"},{"key":"c","text":"Project is ahead of schedule and over budget"},{"key":"d","text":"None of the above"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"SPI = EV/PV &lt; 1 means behind schedule, and CPI = EV/AC &lt; 1 means over budget.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":40,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q40 · 1 mark"},"topic":"AALL1004"},{"id":"PAST-14-041","src":"PAST-14-041","text":"Risk in project management is defined as","options":[{"key":"a","text":"an uncertain event that, if it occurs, has a positive effect on project objectives"},{"key":"b","text":"an uncertain event that, if it occurs, has a negative effect on project objectives"},{"key":"c","text":"an uncertain event that, if it occurs, has a positive or negative effect on project objectives"},{"key":"d","text":"an uncertain event that does not have any effect on project objectives"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"PMBOK defines risk as an uncertain event or condition that, if it occurs, has a positive (opportunity) or negative (threat) effect on project objectives.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":41,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q41 · 1 mark"},"topic":"AALL1004"},{"id":"PAST-14-042","src":"PAST-14-042","text":"In national competitive bidding, the notice shall be published in a national newspaper giving a period of at least","options":[{"key":"a","text":"15 days"},{"key":"b","text":"30 days"},{"key":"c","text":"35 days"},{"key":"d","text":"45 days"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"Under Nepal's Public Procurement Regulation, national competitive bidding allows at least 30 days for bid submission. International bidding needs 45 days.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":42,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q42 · 1 mark"},"topic":"AALL1004"},{"id":"PAST-14-044","src":"PAST-14-044","text":"NEC submits its report to the","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Government of Nepal"},{"key":"b","text":"Prime Minister's Office"},{"key":"c","text":"Ministry of Physical Infrastructure"},{"key":"d","text":"All of the above"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"The Nepal Engineering Council submits its annual report to the Government of Nepal, as the NEC Act requires.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":44,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q44 · 1 mark"},"topic":"AALL1006"},{"id":"PAST-14-052","src":"PAST-14-052","text":"Which one of the following is an example of a regulatory body (instrument)?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Company Act"},{"key":"b","text":"Intellectual Property Right"},{"key":"c","text":"Building codes and bylaws"},{"key":"d","text":"All of the above"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"Company law, intellectual property rights and building codes or bylaws all regulate engineering practice and business. The key takes all three as regulatory.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":52,"marks":1,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q52 · 1 mark"},"topic":"AALL1006"},{"id":"PAST-14-068","src":"PAST-14-068","text":"The optimistic time is 15 days and the pessimistic time is 30 days. The standard deviation (of the activity) is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"2.9"},{"key":"b","text":"2.6"},{"key":"c","text":"2.5"},{"key":"d","text":"2.1"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"In PERT, σ = (t<sub>p</sub> − t<sub>o</sub>)/6 = (30 − 15)/6 = 2.5 days.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":68,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q68 · 2 marks"},"topic":"AALL1003"},{"id":"PAST-14-069","src":"PAST-14-069","text":"Activities A, B and C are the immediate predecessors of activity Y. If the earliest finishing times of the three activities are 12, 15 and 10, then what will be the earliest starting time for Y?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"10"},{"key":"b","text":"15"},{"key":"c","text":"12"},{"key":"d","text":"Cannot be determined"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"Y can start only after all its predecessors finish, so EST(Y) = max(12, 15, 10) = 15.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":69,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q69 · 2 marks"},"topic":"AALL1003"},{"id":"PAST-14-072","src":"PAST-14-072","text":"If the nominal rate of interest is 10% per annum and there is quarterly compounding, the effective rate of interest will be","options":[{"key":"a","text":"10% per annum"},{"key":"b","text":"10.10% per annum"},{"key":"c","text":"10.25% per annum"},{"key":"d","text":"10.38% per annum"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"i<sub>eff</sub> = (1 + 0.10/4)<sup>4</sup> − 1 = 1.025<sup>4</sup> − 1 ≈ 10.38%.","marks":2,"source":{"kind":"past","set":14,"question":72,"marks":2,"date":"2080 Chaitra 23","shift":"3rd shift","reference":"Past Paper Set 14, Q72 · 2 marks"},"topic":"AALL1002"}]}]};
